1
00:00:00,320 --> 00:00:04,800
Alex has lost his headphone case in the library, and
he’s already late for his bus.

2
00:00:04,880 --> 00:00:08,720
Somewhere nearby, Ava finds a case and wants to help.

3
00:00:08,770 --> 00:00:10,560
Getting it back should be simple.

4
00:00:10,560 --> 00:00:15,280
The usual solution is a post in the campus group: what
was found, and where.

5
00:00:15,456 --> 00:00:18,400
Alex spots it and replies beneath the message.

6
00:00:18,580 --> 00:00:21,680
Now the finder and the possible owner are connected.

7
00:00:21,792 --> 00:00:23,360
That’s a useful start.

8
00:00:23,496 --> 00:00:25,680
But a black case is a common description.

9
00:00:25,880 --> 00:00:29,200
The post doesn’t tell the librarian whose it is.

10
00:00:29,280 --> 00:00:34,800
Someone still has to ask the right questions, check the
answers, and arrange collection.

11
00:00:34,944 --> 00:00:38,880
That handoff is where a helpful conversation can get
stuck.

12
00:00:39,149 --> 00:00:41,280
FindBack carries that conversation forward.

13
00:00:41,440 --> 00:00:52,720
It connects the original post, the claimant’s private
chat, and the librarian’s review—giving each person a
clear next step while keeping the final decision with
the librarian.

14
00:00:52,997 --> 00:00:57,680
FindBack continues in Alex’s private chat and asks him
to confirm what he lost.

15
00:00:57,808 --> 00:01:02,640
Then it asks an open question about distinguishing
marks or contents.

16
00:01:02,680 --> 00:01:05,760
The desk’s identifying answers stay private.

17
00:01:05,827 --> 00:01:12,320
After struggling to remember at first, Alex describes a
red thread on the zipper and initials inside the case.

18
00:01:12,420 --> 00:01:16,160
His own description becomes the evidence the librarian
can review.

19
00:01:16,160 --> 00:01:20,360
The agent takes that description to Mira’s private
review conversation.

20
00:01:20,480 --> 00:01:25,680
It brings Alex’s words together with the desk’s record
and explains the possible match.

21
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:30,880
Mira checks the details and approves this specific case
for Alex.

22
00:01:30,980 --> 00:01:35,760
Her approval authorizes collection, and the agent then
reserves the item.

23
00:01:36,029 --> 00:01:43,280
Alex receives a private confirmation: Mira has approved
his request, and his case is reserved at the library
desk.

24
00:01:43,340 --> 00:01:47,920
The collection instructions tell him to ask for Mira
and show his recovery chat.

25
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:50,480
He now knows where to go and what to do.

26
00:01:50,548 --> 00:01:53,280
The found-item post has become an approved pickup.

27
00:01:53,400 --> 00:01:59,360
The evidence and decision stay connected, and Alex
doesn’t have to carry the conversation between people.

28
00:01:59,417 --> 00:02:03,280
Switch connects the group, the private chats, and the
agents.

29
00:02:03,467 --> 00:02:07,600
AI coordinates the handoff; the librarian stays in
control.
